\begin{cases}
a + (x+4) > 3x \\
a + 3x > x+9 \\
(x+4) + 3x > a
\end{cases}
\] Simplifying these inequalities, we get: \[
\begin{cases}
a > 2x-4 \\
a > 9-2x \\
a < 4x+4
\end{cases}
\] Since $a$ is the largest angle, we have $a > b$ and $a > c$. This means that $a > x+9$ and $a > x+4$. Combining these with the previous inequalities, we get: \[
\begin{cases}
2x-4 < a < 4x+4 \\
9-2x < a < x+9 \\
x+4 < a
\end{cases}
\] Solving these inequalities simultaneously, we find that the least possible value of $n-m$ is $\frac{11}{3}$. </think> <answer> $\frac{11}{3}$ </answer>

